1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,820
Do you ever have a task that needs doing, but however urgent it is, you just don't get down to it?
你是否曾经有一项需要完成的任务，但是不管它有多紧急，你就是不着手去做呢？

2
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:15,390
From doing the housework to writing an assignment, there's always something I can put off until tomorrow.
从做家务到写作业，总是有些事情我可以推迟到明天再做。

3
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:19,160
Being a procrastinator is quite easy.
成为一个拖延者很容易。

4
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:28,760
There are so many other more interesting distractions such as watching funny cat videos on the internet or catching up with friends on social media!
还有很多其他更有趣的让人分心的事物，比如在互联网上看看有趣的猫咪视频，或者在社交媒体上和朋友们叙旧！

5
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:49,150
But Benjamin Franklin, one of the Founding Fathers of the United States, once wisely said that: 'You may delay, but time will not', in other words, the time we waste now is gone forever - so maybe I should pull my socks up and get on with what I should be doing.
但是美国开国元勋之一的本杰明·富兰克林曾经明智地说过：“你可以拖延，但时间不会”，换句话说，我们现在浪费的时间一去不复返了——所以也许我应该振作起来，继续去做我应该做的事情。

6
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:56,430
The reason why many of us delay doing something is that we live for the present not the future.
我们很多人拖延做某事的原因是我们为当下而活，而不是为未来而活。

7
00:00:56,430 --> 00:01:07,390
We are reluctant to engage in tasks that relate to the future - sorting something out now that will benefit us in the future doesn't seem a priority.
我们不愿意去做那些与未来相关的任务——现在解决一些对未来有益的事情似乎不是优先考虑的事情。

8
00:01:07,390 --> 00:01:28,190
Daphna Oyserman, Professor of Psychology at the University of Southern California, says: "Many of the future things that are very important require that we start now rather than later... Later means we don't have a lot of time left."
南加州大学的心理学教授达夫娜·奥伊瑟曼说：“许多与未来非常重要的事情都要求我们现在就开始行动，而不是等到以后......等到以后意味着我们没有太多时间了。”

9
00:01:28,190 --> 00:01:33,830
So get cracking now rather than have a last-minute panic!
所以现在就去做吧，而不是到最后一刻才惊慌失措！

10
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:46,050
But it's hard to muster any enthusiasm when there are tedious chores to be done, so how can I control my lack of focus and knuckle down to what really needs to be done?
但是，当有乏味的琐事要做时，很难鼓起任何热情，那么我该如何控制自己注意力不集中，并开始认真做真正需要做的事情呢？

11
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:58,900
One idea is temptation bundling - doing two activities together - one that is boring but essential and one that you enjoy but isn't so productive.
一个想法是诱惑捆绑——将两项活动一起做——一项无聊但必不可少，另一项你喜欢但效率不高。

12
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:05,530
Planning is essential too and we should never underestimate how long things will take us.
计划也很重要，我们绝不应低估事情会花费我们多长时间。

13
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:16,240
However, Dr Catherine Loveday from Westminster University warns us about a 'planning fallacy' thinking we can get something done quickly.
然而，威斯敏斯特大学的凯瑟琳·洛夫迪博士警告我们注意“计划谬误”，即认为我们可以快速完成某事。

14
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:23,600
She says "We never seem to get any better at judging how long things will take us."
她说：“我们在判断事情需要多长时间方面似乎从未有所提高。”
